<Base
  title="About — Tomoki Cheung"
  description="Developer in Honolulu. Chinese and Japanese family, a computer science degree in progress, and an interest in where hardware meets software."
>
  <p class="meta">About</p>
  <h1>Tomoki Cheung</h1>

  <div class="prose body">
    <h2>Background</h2>

    <p>
      I was born in Hawai&#699;i to first-generation parents, Chinese on my
      father&rsquo;s side and Japanese on my mother&rsquo;s. My father, his
      brother, and my grandparents left China and started over here without
      speaking English. What they built is the reason I have the options I do.
    </p>

    <p>
      Growing up between those two worlds meant a lot of things had to be worked
      out from scratch. My mother did what she could with the information
      available to her, but as the eldest I was often the one figuring out how
      the school system worked, and I was doing it without a map. That was hard
      at the time. It is also where my habit of checking things myself comes
      from.
    </p>

    <h2>Now</h2>

    <p>
      I am a senior at UH M&#257;noa on the general track for a BS in Computer
      Science, with an AS in Information Technology from Kapi&#699;olani
      Community College. Alongside school I run a small web development studio
      and build sites for businesses in Honolulu.
    </p>

    <p>
      I want to work somewhere hardware and software meet. Reading about that
      intersection is not the same as having built anything on it, so the next
      step is buying parts and finding out what I do not know. I am deciding
      between Raspberry Pi builds and taking an off-the-shelf robot and making
      it do something it was not designed to do.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>

    <p>
      Over the past year I have used large language models to build and ship
      real things, and it has accelerated what I could learn on my own. I think
      of them the way I think about a phone: a tool that became an extension of
      how people operate, quickly enough that most of us never decided to let it
      happen. These systems are heading the same way, and they will shape what
      people see and do.
    </p>

    <p>
      That makes this an odd moment to be starting out. It is harder to know
      what I should be working on. It is also the first time people without a
      particular background can build things that were closed to them five years
      ago, and I would not have believed that version of the future.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is a long way to go before I would call myself good at this. What I
      try to hold onto is not taking output on faith. When a model tells me
      something works, I want to know why it works, and I would rather find out
      I was wrong early than ship something I cannot explain.
    </p>

    <h2>Away from the desk</h2>

    <p>
      I hike Koko Head. I travel to Japan often to see my mother&rsquo;s family,
      and I have been to a handful of prefectures, but Hokkaido is the one that
      feels like home. Sapporo is where I stay, Hakodate is where I would happily
      spend more time, and our honke, the main family household, is in Esashi in
      the northeast. Getting there is an eight hour drive, longer in practice,
      because we stop to catch up with relatives on the way. The family traces
      back to Fukui before that.
    </p>

    <p>The seafood has not gotten old once.</p>

    <p>
      My favourite game is Detroit: Become Human. It is a long way from where we
      are, but between drone development, language models, and the robots coming
      out of Tesla and Figure, parts of it feel less speculative than they did. I
      would rather stay optimistic about that than not.
    </p>
  </div>

